  "caption": "With a violin exam two weeks away, Quinn jumped to six hours of practice a day. The neck and shoulder protested, and the fingers started to feel fizzy.\n\nPlaying means long, still, often one-sided postures plus repetitive fine work. Sudden jumps in practice time are a common trigger, and load usually matters more than perfect posture.\n\nThings that often help: build practice time gradually, take short movement breaks every 20–30 minutes, add two short strength sessions a week for the neck and upper back, and review your setup with your teacher.\n\nPain lasting more than a couple of weeks, or numbness, tingling or weakness in the fingers, is worth getting assessed. Modifying playing is usually better than stopping completely.\n\nOur Sydney CBD and Chatswood physios can help. Book via the link in bio.\n\nGeneral information only — not a substitute for an individual assessment.",
